-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1e97858a-fa63-5466-bfac-59a51c9825b3', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Harvest Gear$q$, $q$The September my father went to help my uncle with his barn,
my sister Lena said she would teach me the old tractor.
She said it like a chore. She was seventeen.
She had better things to do than ride the fender
while her little brother stalled us in the lane.

The first morning I stalled it four times.
Lena did not shout. She just looked at the sky,
which was worse. The clutch was stiff as a gate.
The field of soybeans waited, brown and rattling,
and I could feel the whole season leaning on me.

On the third day she climbed down and walked ahead,
back to me, as if I were not even there.
I eased the clutch. The engine coughed and held.
I followed her slow steps along the rows,
and when she turned, she was almost smiling.

By the end of the week she sat on the porch
and let me run the wagon to the bin alone.
I waved. She raised her coffee cup.
I think she had decided I was worth her mornings.
I think I had decided to deserve it.

When my father came home, he asked who drove.
Lena pointed at me and went back to her book.
That was all. That was everything.
I still remember how the steering wheel felt
the first time it did not feel too big for my hands.$q$, 'draft')
