   $q$In the last stanza, some children cross "with earbuds in, not looking," and the speaker greets them anyway. How does this detail develop the speaker's character and the poem's meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows the speaker is annoyed by rude children, suggesting that the job is thankless.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It shows the speaker has failed to learn from Mr. Ibarra, suggesting that people never change.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"It shows the speaker wants the children to stop using earbuds, suggesting a rule should ban them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It mirrors the speaker's younger self, showing the speaker now offers the patient care once received.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It mirrors the speaker's younger self, showing the speaker now offers the patient care once received.$q$, $q$The children repeat what the speaker once did ("with my earbuds in"), and greeting them anyway shows the speaker has become like Mr. Ibarra, hopeful that "one day" they will respond. The greeting is patient, not annoyed.$q$, 'draft', '12bc1dba-f924-5116-b826-af525ce9d544')
